ne your export policies  so that you can allow certain clients to  read and write  while other clients can only read data  in cloud volumes  so now let's talk through a few use  cases and what we've seen  you know with early customers that are  that are using cloud volumes  so the first one is around continuous  integration and a software development  environment  so we have a customer who has an  automated ci environment  that essentially deploys a cluster with  over a thousand different containers  that come up in that cluster  and all of those containers require  access to a shared file system  to hold all the binaries that the  containers are going to use for their  build jobs as well as the output to  build artifacts once the jobs are  complete  right so again a great use case for  cloud volumes because you get that  shared file service  but the other key thing to note here is  the kpis in this environment  are the amount of unit tests that these  customers can do in a given hour  and that is directly correlated with the  amount of time it takes to do a build  right so it's not just about being able  to provide a shared file system  but being able to provide a shared file  system that is high performance so  they need high throughput they need low  latency to be able to keep those build  times  to the minimum and do more unit tests  within a given  period of time  the next use cases are on video  rendering and this is one of those  classic use cases with media processing  workflows rendering  transcoding both from a product  production and post-production sense  that have tools that are built around  shared file system and the access  pattern here is actually very different  from the earlier use case we talked  about here  access is very serial it is still high  performant but it requires also again  very very low latency this is this is  another good use case we see for cloud  volumes  that matches the tools that are built  for this use case with the performance  that cloud volumes deliver  so earlier manvinder you touched on high  performance computing  and eda so i'm going to use the eda  example here and  eda you know is a complicated  environment on its own you have various  phases around  specification block design and  verification synthesis  formal verification and simulation and  each of these phases has different  tools but and requires different file  access patterns  but this is an example of you know  workloads that have  for years relied on file services so you  have applications and tools  that are designed with the need for file  services and these tools specific  protocols like nfs  exactly and these tools are not going to  get re-architected  right they are going to exist as they  are it's just you know too hard to  rebuild these tools  and so when you have you know front-end  design systems  in this eda space that resemble software  r d they're code heavy  they use verilog they use vhdl and to  your point  these are tools that again are very  tightly integrated with  or require nfs right  and the kpi around here again  performance is of the essence  the ability to complete these workflows  in time so this requires  you know cloud volume specifically to be  able to handle large volumes of small  files at very very low latency  especially especially during the  verification stage  the final use cases around databases  right and we have customers that are  using mysql databases and this could be  you know this is  could be other databases as well for  transactional web platforms and they've  built an application that actually  requires scale-up performance  but the other unique thing around this  or not just around performance is  you know this customer or this set of  customers actually needs the ability to  take  consistent point-in-time reference  copies of their database for recovery  purposes  and again the ability to do this while  not impacting user experience  still providing those high iops and hot  and low latency on a shared file system  are key for this this particular  customer so another great use case for  cloud volumes  awesome that's a lot of use cases yeah  so let's talk about timelines where we  are today and and where we're going to  go with cloud volumes in the future  so currently the service is available in  private preview  we've been running the private preview  since early may since about when we  announced the partnership  and we have a few customers that are  trying cloud volumes are really happy  with it that's right  and we have we're offering this out of  the gcp us east 4 region  for those of you that are watching and  are interested in participating in the  preview and expressing your interest for  cloud volumes  please go to the link that's on the  slide netapp.com forward slash gcp  preview  and register there in terms of future  you know the next phases for the service  are really taking it to control the  availability and general availability  so we're going to be making some  announcements around the google next  time frame  as well as discussing which regions the  service rolls out to  so i encourage all of you please visit  us at the netapp booth at next  connect with our smes and get some  hands-on demos at the booth as well cool  so to quickly summarize cloud volumes  for gcp basically are simple to consume  no ops nfs or smb services they offer  our customers high performance and rich  data management  features and are very tightly integrated  into gcp along different dimensions on  the user interface and control plane  side  with support and with billing and  ultimately cloud volumes for google  allow our customers to migrate existing  workloads to gcp they allow our  customers to develop  new applications on gcp that require  performant file storage  and finally allow our customers to take  advantage of the best in class analytics  and machine learning capabilities that  google offers on unstructured data  awesome thank you ali all right folks  stay tuned for live q a  we'll be back in less than a minute  okay uh so we've got some great  questions here uh i'm gonna go a little  bit out of water alim sure just to touch  upon the  stuff we just talked about um so we  talked about uh  how a lot of the features that customers  are used to on premise uh  with netapp in many cases uh are now  available in the cloud  uh but a question that we're getting a  lot from from our audience is uh how do  i get that data into cloud volumes  i may have it in that app or in a  different platform how do i move that  data yeah so we're actually going to  use our cloud sync tool to be able to  allow customers to move data from  on-premise  into cloud volumes as well as between  cloud volumes  and we can expect that it's not  available now but it'll be a roadmap  item that we add around the ga time  frame  great and that that's uh moving data  over the network right that's correct  yeah that's that's an over the wire  and it doesn't have to be netapp on  premise it can be yeah and then this  this is the you know the great thing  about a tool like cloud sync right it  can work with any unstructured file  format right  it could be netapp storage it could be  other storage it basically as long as  that storage exposes an nfs  endpoint or smb endpoint we're able to  then migrate that data  into cloud volumes any limit on the  amount of data i want to move terabytes  hundreds of terabytes  yeah so this is where again it's a use  case dependent uh question right like if  you have small amounts of data and you  have good network throughput you're able  to move that right  or using tools like cloud sync what we  call over-the-wire synchronization tools  but as you get into larger bulk data  sets we're actually exploring  partnering with google on being able to  move bulk data in an offline fashion  right so more of  a bulk data move and then followed by a  sink to get  you know basically hundreds of terabytes  into gcp into cloud volumes  okay cool awesome uh so uh talking about  the protocols that the service is going  to support  nfs there are multiple versions v3 v4  what's  what's the thinking on that front so  initially we're going to support nfs v3  and smb v3 and i think that kind of  answers a couple of those questions  we're going to add smb sorry nfs v4  support down the  road and this is also an area where we  really want to hear from our customers  right on what kind of protocols they  want to use we're hearing a lot of  demand for smd v3 and nfs v3 which is  why we're starting  that's where bulk of the applications  yeah what they use right yeah i think  based on some of the analysis we did  about 80  of the workloads that are nfs based use  nfs v3  so that seemed like a logical place to  go uh but again we're we're open to  feedback and hearing from customers on  use cases around nfs v4 and what they  want to do  great fantastic is the cloud volume uh  accessible from multiple gcp projects ah  so that we touched a little bit on this  presentation but this is a good question  to bring up  so much like any other gcp resource  within a project  this is a a project level resource so  cloud volume  exists and is accessible only within the  context of a project  right so if you have multiple projects  cloud volume in one project  is only accessible to that project cloud  volumes in another project only  accessible to that other project  right there's no sharing of cloud  volumes between projects  okay great all right what regions is the  service available in  yeah so regions wise we're currently  offering private preview in u.s east 4  however we're going to be rolling out to  other regions in the u.s the timeline  for that is when we will talk about  during the next announcements  in terms of which regions are coming up  and when one of the things i want to add  to that is  if you have workloads that you want to  move to gcp and you have specific  regions in mind  uh please do give us the feedback you  could use the link that we shared  earlier for signing up for the private  preview and  that way we'll connect with you and we'd  love to hear which regions are a  priority for you  so we can we can work on those and  prioritize those accordingly  uh thank you for tuning in everyone uh  that's that's all we had for  this session uh please do stay tuned in  for the next session  which is on connected healthcare and how  to enable secure entry points into the  cloud with chrome  thank you  you
 

